 Meteorological conditions over the study region were characterized using the ERA5 single-level reanalysis dataset from ECMWF. We used the Copernicus Climate Data Store (CDS) product ``ERA5 hourly data on single levels'' with the ``reanalysis'' product option. \textcolor{red}{The original ERA5 product has an hourly temporal resolution. We selected only the hourly values at 00:00, 06:00, 12:00, and 18:00 UTC, giving a 6-hour sampling interval; no 6-hour accumulation, temporal averaging, or temporal interpolation was performed.} Following data-quality screening, the complete period from January 2004 to December 2021 was retained, yielding 26,300 consecutive timestamps with no missing values in the variables used for subsequent analysis. Eleven meteorological variables were initially considered as candidate predictors: 10-metre u-component of wind (u10), 10-metre v-component of wind (v10), 2-metre dewpoint temperature (d2m), 2-metre temperature (t2m), surface pressure (sp), evaporation (e), maximum total precipitation rate (mxtpr), minimum total precipitation rate (mntpr), total cloud cover (tcc), surface net solar radiation (ssr), and large-scale precipitation (lsp). Total precipitation (tp) was used as the prediction target, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:variables}.
 
 For each timestamp, the meteorological variables were spatially averaged across the 25 ERA5 grid points within the Dublin study domain to construct a regional mean time series. Each retained total-precipitation value represents the accumulation during the hour ending at its timestamp. Total precipitation was multiplied by 1000 to convert metres to millimetres. This length-unit conversion does not change the accumulation window. Dividing the one-hour amount by its one-hour duration gives the hourly-mean rate in mm\,h$^{-1}$; its numerical value is unchanged. The prediction target and all precipitation-valued forecast metrics are reported in mm\,h$^{-1}$ throughout. No temporal interpolation or forward-filling was applied because the retained 2004--2021 period formed a complete and continuous 6-hourly time series. The resulting regional time series was chronologically divided into training (70\%), validation (15\%), and testing (15\%) subsets to preserve temporal ordering and prevent future observations from influencing model development. Standardization was performed using statistics estimated exclusively from the training subset, and the same transformation was subsequently applied to the validation and test subsets.
+
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,filecolor=red,urlcolor=red}
+The main 2004--2021 experiment contains 18,410/3,945/3,945 raw training/validation/test timestamps and 18,404/3,939/3,939 forecast targets after six-snapshot windows are constructed separately within each partition. The target-time ranges (UTC) are 2 January 2004 12:00--7 August 2016 06:00 for training, 9 August 2016 00:00--20 April 2019 12:00 for validation, and 22 April 2019 06:00--31 December 2021 18:00 for testing. These are forecast target times, not input-window start times. The 1992--2003 extension is used only in the supplementary rolling-origin evaluation.
+\par}
 
