 For the retrospective input sequence, every predictor has a valid time at or before the forecast origin $t_0$. Large-scale precipitation (lsp) is accumulated over the hour ending at its valid time, while mxtpr and mntpr are the maximum and minimum total precipitation rates within that hour. These precipitation inputs and the tp target come from ERA5 short forecasts initialized at 06 and 18~UTC; the instantaneous inputs sp, tcc, v10 and d2m are supplied as analysis fields in the CDS product \citep{ecmwf_era5_documentation}. Thus, the latest precipitation input represents $(t_0-1\,\mathrm{h},t_0]$, whereas the target represents $(t_0+5\,\mathrm{h},t_0+6\,\mathrm{h}]$; no target-hour precipitation value is directly included in the input window. At 00 and 12~UTC origins, however, the latest precipitation input and target are steps 6 and 12 of the same short-forecast cycle. Feature screening and standardization use training-period data only. Valid-time ordering does not establish that the reanalysis inputs were available in real time at $t_0$.
