+\subsubsection{Retrospective Rolling-Origin Evaluation}
+\label{sec:rolling_origin_methods}
+
+We conducted a retrospective rolling-origin robustness evaluation using the extended 1992--2021 ERA5 archive. Expanding training periods were 1992--2007, 1992--2010, 1992--2013 and 1992--2016; their validation periods were 2008--2009, 2011--2012, 2014--2015 and 2017--2018, and their non-overlapping test periods were 2010--2012, 2013--2015, 2016--2018 and 2019--2021, respectively (F1--F4). F4$\prime$ instead trained on 2004--2016 and shared F4\textquotesingle s validation and test targets, isolating the addition of 1992--2003 training history within this protocol. The longest training history was 25 years; F1 and F2 used 16 and 19 years. Six models and five fixed seeds (42, 123, 7, 2024, 99) yielded 150 runs. The six models were XGBoost, Transformer, BiLSTM-Attention, BU-LSTM, BU-LSTM-Attention and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention; the plain BiLSTM control was not included.
+
+Scalers, Q95, month-by-hour climatology and AR(1) coefficients were estimated from each fold\textquotesingle s training data only. A training-only check of the original 11-candidate contemporaneous screening rule recovered the same seven predictors in every fold; training retained that fixed set. Windows did not cross partition boundaries. Architectures and hyperparameters were reused from the original study, without fold-specific retuning; recurrent models retained the 30-epoch cap and validation-based early stopping. This preserves temporal separation at the preprocessing and model-fitting stages but does not make model-design choices independent of these retrospectively selected periods.
+
+Rolling-origin RMSE comparisons used a paired non-circular moving-block bootstrap: 28 six-hourly samples (7 days), 5,000 replicates and resampling seed 42, with additional pooled checks at 56 and 120 samples (14 and 30 days). Blocks were sampled on the full chronological sequence before wet-subset masking; each replicate used $\lceil n/L\rceil$ complete blocks of length $L$, without truncating its final block. Common sampled indices were applied across compared forecasts and seeds; RMSE differences were calculated per seed and then averaged, rather than averaging predictions first. Pooled comparisons concatenated F1--F4 targets before scoring and can sample blocks across the short fold-boundary gaps. All intervals are pointwise 95\% percentile intervals without multiplicity adjustment. Paired seed-$t$ intervals for the history comparison separately describe training variability across five seeds (four degrees of freedom); neither interval combines both uncertainty sources.
+
+Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_design}--\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_coverage} provide configuration counts, performance, paired comparisons, history effects and conditional coverage, separately from the main experiment. F4$\prime$ uses 13 complete calendar training years and is not the original 70/15/15 main split; it does not replace Table~\ref{tab:seed_stability}.
+\par}
