+\subsubsection{Retrospective Rolling-Origin Robustness}
+\label{sec:rolling_origin_results}
+
+Across the four rolling test periods, all six learning models retained positive RMSE skill relative to training-fitted month-by-hour climatology (0.077--0.125). AR(1) also had positive skill (0.031--0.055), whereas Persistence had negative skill in every period. XGBoost had the numerically lowest RMSE in F1--F3, and BiLSTM-Attention in F4; pairwise Kendall rank correlations among the six-model rankings ranged from 0.60 to 0.87. Only F1\textquotesingle s BiLSTM-Attention--XGBoost all-target interval clearly excluded zero among the four period-specific comparisons. These results do not identify a uniformly best model.
+
+Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_design} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_performance} report all configurations and forecasts; Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_pairs_all} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_pairs_wet} retain all saved paired comparisons.
+
+On the 17,508 pooled test targets, the mean seed-level RMSE difference (BiLSTM-Attention minus XGBoost) was $+0.00204$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Its 7-day interval was $[+0.00007,+0.00398]$, its 14-day interval $[-0.00003,+0.00402]$, and its 30-day interval $[+0.00006,+0.00404]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The small all-target separation is therefore sensitive to block length. On the distinct wet subset ($\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$; $n=3,610$), the difference was $+0.0093$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ with a 7-day interval of $[+0.0048,+0.0137]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, supporting lower XGBoost RMSE within that subset rather than an overall superiority claim.
+
+Block-length sensitivity is given in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_block_sensitivity}.
