 \subsection{Limitations and Future Work}
 \label{sec:limitations}
+
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,filecolor=red,urlcolor=red}
+The rolling-origin analysis is retrospective rather than a fully prospective or fully independent validation of model-design choices. The original training targets extended through 7 August 2016 and validation targets through 20 April 2019; these development data overlap the F1--F3 test years. F4 begins on 2 January 2019, so its early-2019 targets also overlap the original validation period; the original test targets begin only on 22 April 2019. The original test portion was not used for initial hyperparameter selection but had already informed the original model comparisons. Fold-specific fitting and preprocessing therefore do not remove development-stage overlap. Models were not retuned per fold; F1/F2 had only 16/19 training years. No ENSO-phase stratification, rolling plain-BiLSTM control or rolling Bernoulli--Gamma alternative was evaluated. Pooled bootstrap blocks can cross the 42-hour gaps between adjacent fold target sequences. These boundaries limit claims of temporal independence, attention-specific benefit and robustness to distributional formulation.
+\par}
 
 Several limitations affect the interpretation and generalization of the present results. The first concerns the spatial representation of precipitation. All meteorological variables, including the prediction target, were spatially averaged across the 25 ERA5 grid points within the study domain. The resulting forecasts therefore represent Dublin regional mean precipitation rather than rainfall at individual locations. \textcolor{red}{Spatial averaging provides a compact regional forecasting problem but can smooth localized rainfall maxima and reduce the apparent intensity of small-scale precipitation events. Consequently, upper-tail precipitation events ($\geq$Q95) of the ERA5 regional mean must not be equated with heavy rainfall measured at an individual station. This distinction limits the interpretation of the upper-tail results as local high-impact rainfall forecasts.}
 
