 %----------------------------------------------------------
 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
+
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,filecolor=red,urlcolor=red}
+BiLSTM-Attention achieved the numerically lowest RMSE on the original test period, but its advantage over XGBoost was small and did not persist consistently across the rolling-origin evaluation. The supplementary retrospective evaluation retained positive climatological skill for all six models evaluated there in each test period, but did not remove upper-tail detection and interval-coverage deficits; it does not establish prospective independence of the model-design choices.
+\par}
 
 This study presented a systematic evaluation of machine-learning and deep-learning approaches for short-term regional precipitation forecasting over Dublin using hourly ERA5 data from 2004 to 2021 subsampled at 6-hour intervals to predict hourly regional-mean precipitation at a 6-hour lead time. The analysis considered deterministic and probabilistic forecasting, repeated-run robustness, rainfall-regime performance, event-based evaluation, and model interpretation. Its conclusions concern ERA5-derived regional-mean precipitation; the observational checks do not establish equivalent station-forecast skill. Rather than focusing on a single model architecture, the study examined how different modelling choices affect forecast accuracy and reliability under a common experimental setting.
 
